    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Hawaiian religion is polytheistic and animistic: Kāne, Kū, Lono and Kanaloa are especially prominent among many gods, and every Native Hawaiian family was considered to have one or more guardian spirits, the ʻaumākua.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapu-hawaiian-culture">kapu</a> system, a set of taboos separating the spiritually pure from the potentially unclean, ruled daily life until 1819, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-ii">Kamehameha II</a>, persuaded by two of his father's wives and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahuna">kahuna</a> nui <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hewahewa">Hewahewa</a>, overthrew it.</li><li>Missionaries arrived from 1820 and most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alii">aliʻi</a> converted, but family ʻaumākua, veneration of bones and sacred places survived, and a Hawaiian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> in the 1970s brought a resurgence of the religion.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumulipo">Kumulipo</a>, the man Kiʻi succeeds in seducing and reproducing with the woman Laʻilaʻi before the god Kāne has the chance, so the divine lineage becomes younger than, and subservient to, the lineage of humankind.</p></aside>
    </section>

