    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hawaiian religion comprises the Indigenous religious beliefs and practices of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/native-hawaiians">Native Hawaiians</a>, and is also known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapu-hawaiian-culture">kapu</a> system. It was based partly on the tapu system common in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a> and likely originated among the Tahitians and other Pacific Islanders who landed in Hawaiʻi between 500 and 1300 CE. It is unclear when settlers first came, but there is significant evidence that the islands were settled no later than 800 CE and that immigration continued to about 1300 CE. Settlers came from the Marquesas and greater Polynesia, and at some point a significant influx of Tahitians brought their religious beliefs with them.</p><p>Early Hawaiian religion resembled other Polynesian religions in being largely focused on natural forces such as the tides, the sky and volcanic activity, and on human dependence on nature for subsistence. The major early gods reflected this: early Hawaiians worshipped Kāne, god of the sky and creation, Kū, god of war and male pursuits, Lono, god of peace, rain and fertility, and Kanaloa, god of the ocean.</p><p>The people were spread among eight islands, with waves of immigration over hundreds of years, so religious practices evolved over time and from place to place. The scholar Mary Kawena Pukui, raised in Kaʻū, maintained that the early Hawaiian gods were benign.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hawaiian religion is polytheistic, with a belief in many deities and spirits, including the animistic belief that spirits are found in non-human animals and natural elements such as the waves and the sky. Kāne, Kū, Lono and Kanaloa are especially prominent; other notable deities include Laka, Kihawahine, Haumea, Papahānaumoku and, most famously, Pele. Every Native Hawaiian family was considered to have one or more guardian spirits, the ʻaumākua. The historian June Gutmanis sorted the pantheon into six groups, from the four gods (ka hā) to the guardians, while Jay Kauka described three: the four gods, many lesser gods (kupua) tied to professions, and the ʻaumakua of families. Not all ancient Hawaiians believed in deities: some were atheists, called ʻaia.</p><p>Prayer was an essential part of life, said when building a house, making a canoe or giving lomilomi massage. Lono was worshipped during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makahiki">Makahiki</a> season and Kū in times of war. The usual posture, sitting upright with head high and eyes open, has been read as a relationship of respect and self-respect, with the ʻaumākua bridging the gap between gods and people.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heiau">Heiau</a> were the focal points for prayer. Offerings, sacrifices and prayers were made at these temples, at thousands of koʻa (shrines), at many wahi pana (sacred places) and at small kuahu (altars) in homes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hawaiian tradition is oral. One creation myth is embodied in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumulipo">Kumulipo</a>, an epic chant that links the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alii">aliʻi</a>, or Hawaiian royalty, to the gods. It is divided into two sections: night, or pō, which corresponds to divinity, and day, or ao, which corresponds to humankind. After the birth of Laʻilaʻi, the woman, and Kiʻi, the man, the man succeeds in seducing and reproducing with the woman before the god Kāne has a chance, making the divine lineage younger than, and subservient to, the lineage of humankind. This is read as the passage of humankind from being symbols for the gods (the literal meaning of kiʻi) into keepers of those symbols in the form of idols and the like. The Kumulipo was recited at the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makahiki">Makahiki</a>, to honour Lono, the god of fertility.</p><p>Other voices come through prayer and spirit-speech. Histories of the 19th century describe prayer throughout the day, with set prayers for sleeping, eating, drinking and travelling. In 1867 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahuna">kahuna</a> Kūpaʻiulu of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> described a counter-sorcery ritual to heal someone made ill by hoʻopiʻopiʻo, another person's evil thoughts: a kapa cloth was shaken and prayers were said, and if the evil spirit appeared and possessed the patient, the practitioner could save them by speaking with it.</p><p>Writers on the tradition include Pukui, and Pali Jae Lee, who describes the ancient time as one of family and oneness with all things.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i.jpg" alt="Kamehameha I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_Choris_(1795-1828)_-_Kamehameha,_King_of_the_Sandwich_Islands_by_Louis_Choris,_(Russian),_pen_and_watercolor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Louis Choris, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hawaiian religion came to the islands with settlers from the Marquesas and greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a> and with a significant influx of Tahitians, in the centuries to about 1300 CE. Tradition holds that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapu-hawaiian-culture">kapu</a> system arrived with Pāʻao, a priest or chief from Tahiti who came around 1200 CE.</p><p>The kapu system stayed in place until 1819. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha the Great</a> that year, two of his wives, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ka-ahumanu">Kaʻahumanu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keopuolani">Keōpūolani</a>, then the most powerful people in the kingdom, conferred with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahuna">kahuna</a> nui <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hewahewa">Hewahewa</a>. They persuaded the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-ii">Liholiho</a>, Kamehameha II, to overthrow it, and the people were ordered to burn the wooden statues and tear down the rock temples. Without the hierarchical system in place, some abandoned the old gods, while others kept worshipping them, especially their family ʻaumākua.</p><p>Protestant Christian missionaries arrived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from 1820 and eventually gained great political, moral and economic influence in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>. Most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alii">aliʻi</a>, among them Kaʻahumanu and Keōpūolani, converted. It took 11 years for Kaʻahumanu to proclaim laws against ancient religious practices, forbidding the worship of idols and ancient gods, the hula, chant and song, and the planting and drinking of ʻawa.</p><p>In the 1970s the religion experienced a resurgence during the Hawaiian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahuna">kahuna</a> were respected, educated individuals who formed a class in the social hierarchy, serving the King and the courtiers and assisting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makaainana">makaʻāinana</a>, the common people. Kahuna were often healers, navigators, builders, prophets or temple workers and philosophers, and they also talked with the spirits. Pukui and others believed that kahuna did not have the mystical transcendent experiences described in other religions: possession was a trance-like state but a communion with known spirits.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapu-hawaiian-culture">Kapu</a> was a system of taboos designed to separate the spiritually pure (laʻa) from the potentially unclean (haumia). It imposed restrictions on daily life: the separation of men and women at mealtimes (ʻaikapu), restrictions on gathering and preparing food, the separation of menstruating women, restrictions on looking at, touching or being close to chiefs and people of known spiritual power, and limits on overfishing. Women were forbidden to eat pig, coconut, banana and certain red foods because of their male symbolism.</p><p>Punishments for breaking the kapu could include death, though a person who escaped to a puʻuhonua, a city of refuge, could be saved. The kahuna nui mandated long periods of absolute silence in the village, when no baby could cry, dog howl or rooster crow on pain of death. In wartime the first two men killed were offered to the gods, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a> was not unknown.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapu-hawaiian-culture">kapu</a> fell and the missionaries came, a number of traditions survived through integration, practice in hiding or practice in rural communities. They include the worship of family ancestral gods, the ʻaumākua, the veneration of iwi (bones) and the preservation of wahi pana, sacred places. Hula, at one time outlawed as a religious practice, is today performed in both spiritual and secular contexts. Some Hawaiians practise Christianised versions of old traditions, and others practise the old faith as a co-religion. Today Hawaiian religious practices are protected by the American Indian Religious Freedom Act.</p><p>In the 1930s the American author Max Freedom Long originated a philosophy and practice he called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huna-people">Huna</a>. Long and his successors represent it as a type of ancient Hawaiian occultism, but the scholars Rothstein and Chai consider it a New Age mix of cultural appropriation and fantasy, not representative of traditional Hawaiian religion.</p><p>Traditional beliefs have played a role in the politics of Hawaiʻi since contact. In 1976 the suit Aluli et al. v. Brown forced the Navy to survey and protect important sites on Kahoʻolawe and allow limited access for religious purposes. After 1,000 graves, dating back to 850 CE, were unearthed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> in 1988, a hotel was moved inland. Since 2014 protests have continued over Mauna Kea, which some Hawaiians regard as the most sacred mountain of their religion; practitioners have repeatedly failed in court to prove that these practices predate 1893.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Hawaiian religion, also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapu-hawaiian-culture">kapu</a> system, is the polytheistic, animistic faith of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/native-hawaiians">Native Hawaiians</a>, with Kāne, Kū, Lono and Kanaloa among its chief gods and family guardian spirits called ʻaumākua. The kapu system was overthrown in 1819; traditions survived through families and sacred places and revived from the 1970s.</em></p>

